integrated_rules:
Ban phones in schools if (A) phone presence materially increases students’ exposure to immediate, real-time harmful peer interactions (e.g., cyberbullying/harassment) such that the expected likelihood of conflict escalation to physical harm is meaningfully higher, and (B) phone presence also meaningfully undermines students’ focus/learning (e.g., distraction/mental load leading to worse educational performance), and (C) these safety/education harms cannot be adequately prevented by less-than-total restrictions such that emergency communication and required contact can still occur quickly through non-phone alternatives; 

Oppose a ban if (A) keeping phones materially reduces avoidable risk in genuine emergencies by enabling fast, reliable communication that families/students depend on, and (B) removing phones would substantially impair that rapid contact (because workable alternatives would cause delays or failures), and (C) the educational/behavioral goal of teaching self-control and digital citizenship is better achieved through managed use rather than elimination; 

If both the safety/learning harms from phone presence and the emergency/learning-education harms from a ban are significant and neither can be shown to be sufficiently mitigated without a full ban, then the record is insufficient to justify either a full ban or no ban.
